course: dictionary
lesson: 8573
title: The sceptic
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A sceptic is a person who doubts something and wants evidence before believing it. Sceptic is a noun, and sceptical is the adjective, as in "sceptical about" a claim. Context helps here: «Sceptics doubt that miracles happen» sits next to the word doubt, so a sceptic is someone who does not believe easily.
explain: A sceptic does not say "that is false" straight away. They ask, "Where is the proof?" A good scientist is a healthy sceptic, testing claims before accepting them. Online, a sceptic checks a video before sharing it, because some posts are fake news.
explain: Being a sceptic does not mean being rude. «Christians and non-Christians can discuss this with respect.» People can disagree about what is true and still listen politely to each other.
fact: A sceptic (noun) is a person who doubts something and wants evidence before believing it.
fact: Sceptical (adjective) means doubtful, or not ready to believe something yet.
fact: You can be a sceptic about one idea, like "a climate sceptic", or about religious beliefs.
fact: «Sceptics doubt that miracles happen» means some people do not believe miracles happen, while Christians believe God can act in the world.
fact: Asking for evidence is a healthy habit, and it helps you check claims before you believe them.
exercise: «Sceptics doubt that miracles happen.» What is a sceptic? | *Someone who doubts and wants evidence before believing | A kind of religious leader | Someone who believes everything they hear | A type of weather
exercise: Ben says, "Show me the proof before I believe it." Which word best describes Ben? | Gardener | *Sceptic | Thief | Giant
exercise: "Mia is sceptical about the claim that the lake has a monster." What does sceptical mean here? | Excited and certain | Brave and bold | *Doubtful, not ready to believe | Hungry for food
test: Which word names a person who doubts and asks for evidence? | Believer | *Sceptic | Dreamer | Optimist
test: Why is a sceptic useful when you see fake news online? | *Because they check the evidence before sharing | Because they believe every headline | Because they share everything quickly | Because they never use the internet
exam: «Christians and non-Christians can discuss this with respect.» What does this show about sceptics? | They stop asking questions | They must always win the argument | *They can disagree politely and still listen | They never talk to people who believe
